From 6a30c47ef8bab772ef0124891e60ced8220d2d93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:39:40 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Make constructor purity part of the Zinc API

Clients compile against whether a class has initialization code.
`Namer` makes the primary constructor of a class `StableRealizable`
iff every base class is `NoInits` (`setStableConstructor`), and
`TreeInfo.isPureApply` then treats `new C` as pure. `refPurity` does
the same for a reference to an object whose module class is
`isNoInitsRealClass`, as does `isPureSyntheticCaseApply` for `C(...)`.
A pure argument to an unused parameter of an inline method is elided,
and a pure statement draws the "pure expression does nothing" warning
and compiles to nothing: a bare `O` against an object without
initializers emits no module load.

Adding a statement or a private val to a template flips that status
without changing any signature, so Zinc recompiles only the edited
class. A client compiled earlier keeps the elision, and so the
initializer silently never runs. Descendants also keep a stale `STABLE`
in their TASTy, which clients compiled later read. This reproduces on
stock Zinc with name hashing and Scala 3.9.0:

```
class A                       // later: class A { Counter.n += 1 }
class B extends A
object X {
  inline def ignore(x: Any): Unit = ()
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    ignore(new B)
    assert(Counter.n == 1, "B's constructor was elided")
  }
}
```

This commit adds a `<pure-init>` marker annotation to the API of the
primary constructor of each class without initialization code, in the
same way as we already fold inline bodies into the API. A flip changes
the name hash of the constructor (`p;C;init;`). For traits, we mark
when all base classes are `NoInits`, rather than the trait's own
`StableRealizable`, which reflects its body alone: `new T {}` in a
client depends on all of them.

On the dependency side, `new C` already uses the constructor's name.
A reference to an object `O` did not, so `ExtractDependencies` now
records the name of `O`'s constructor for each module reference, and
that of `C`'s constructor for a synthetic case class `apply`. Those are
precisely the inputs to `refPurity` and `isPureSyntheticCaseApply`. I
preferred that over marking the class itself, whose simple name is used
by every client mentioning the type.

The constructor of an object was dropped from its declared members as
"always present", but it came back among the inherited members, since
`baseClasses` includes the class itself. I've removed the filter so the
constructor and its marker are declared.

Descendants need nothing more. Under name hashing an API change of `A`
invalidates its transitive descendants, and each recompiled descendant
gets a fresh marker on its own constructor, which invalidates its own
clients in the next cycle.

The first build with this compiler recompiles everything, as with any
change to the extracted API. Flips should be rare afterwards: none of
the edits in the pekko campaign I used to evaluate Merkle hashing
changes any class's init status.

Testing: `ExtractAPISpecification` checks that the constructor's name
hash changes when an ancestor gains a template statement, a private val
or a trait val, and stays the same for unrelated edits.
`IncrementalCompileSimulatorSpecification` checks that clients doing
`ignore(new B)`, `{ O; 1 }` and `ignore(C(1))` are invalidated in both
directions. The new tests fail without the change. The
`ExtractUsedNamesSpecification` expectations now include the
constructor names of referenced objects.

I also ran a Zinc scripted test (develop, name hashing) against a local
build of this commit: a client doing `ignore(new B)`, `O` and
`ignore(C(1))` is compiled, then `A` gains `Counter.n += 1`. With the
3.9.0 bridge, only `A` recompiles and the client sees `Counter.n == 0`.
With this commit, Zinc recompiles `B`, `C` and `O` (cycle 2) and then
the client (cycle 3), and it sees 3:

```
[inv] Cycle 1 API changes
[inv]     A: modified names A;init;
[inv] Cycle 2 API changes
[inv]     B: modified names A;init;, B;init;
[inv]     C: modified names A;init;, C;init;
[inv]     O: modified names A;init;, O$;init;
[inv] Member-reference invalidation of X
[inv]   modified names used by the class:
[inv]     B;init;
```
